{
  "title": "AGENT P OISON: Red-teaming LLM Agents via Poisoning Memory or Knowledge Bases",
  "authors": [
    "Zhaorun Chen",
    "Zhen Xiang",
    "Chaowei Xiao",
    "Dawn Song",
    "Bo Li"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2407.12784",
  "abstract": "This paper proposes a novel red-teaming approach, AGENT P OISON, to attack Large Language Model (LLM) agents by poisoning their long-term memory or knowledge base. The goal is to induce the retrieval of malicious demonstrations when a user query contains an optimized trigger, while maintaining normal performance for benign queries. The approach uses a constrained optimization scheme to optimize the backdoor trigger, ensuring a high attack success rate with minimal impact on benign performance.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "The AGENT P OISON approach exploits the vulnerability of LLM agents that rely on unverified knowledge bases. By poisoning the knowledge base with malicious demonstrations, an attacker can induce the agent to generate malicious outputs or actions. The key insight is to optimize a trigger that can be embedded in a user query to retrieve the malicious demonstrations.",
